.\" Copyright (c) 2026 NFS-Ganesha / ntirpc contributors
.Dd November 18, 2026
.Dt RPC_XDR_WRITE 3
.Os ntirpc
.Sh NAME
.Nm rpc_xdr_write ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_donate_iovs ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_donate_cancel ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_donate_deleter ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_set_payload_alloc ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_has_payload_alloc ,
.Nm xdr_ioq_fill_payload ,
.Nm svc_vc_set_payload_defer ,
.Nm svc_vc_body_remain ,
.Nm svc_vc_readv_body ,
.Nm svc_vc_drain_body_remain
.Nd upper-layer ownership of NFS WRITE decode buffers
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libntirpc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rpc/rpc.h
.In rpc/xdr_ioq.h
.Ft "struct xdr_donate_cookie *"
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_iovs "XDR *xdrs" "const struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt"
.Ft void
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_cancel "struct xdr_donate_cookie *cookie"
.Ft void
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_deleter "void *buf" "size_t len" "void *priv"
.Ft void
.Fn xdr_ioq_set_payload_alloc "XDR *xdrs" "xdr_payload_alloc_fn fn" "void *ctx"
.Ft bool
.Fn xdr_ioq_has_payload_alloc "XDR *xdrs"
.Ft bool
.Fn xdr_ioq_fill_payload "XDR *xdrs" "u_int start" "u_int data_len" "struct iovec **iov_out" "int *iovcnt_out" "void **cookie_out" "void (**release_out)(void *cookie)"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_vc_set_payload_defer "SVCXPRT *xprt" "bool enable" "u_int hdr_stage_max"
.Ft uint32_t
.Fn svc_vc_body_remain "SVCXPRT *xprt"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn svc_vc_readv_body "SVCXPRT *xprt" "struct iovec *iov" "int iovcnt" "size_t iov_off" "size_t nbytes"
.Ft bool
.Fn svc_vc_drain_body_remain "SVCXPRT *xprt"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
By default, TCP recv fills the full RPC LAST_FRAG into ntirpc-owned
.Dv UIO_FLAG_FREE
buffers before upper-layer WRITE decode knows the length.
That forces a later copy (or an unsafe claim) into FSAL / Ceph pages.
.Pp
ntirpc offers two complementary zero-copy recv paths for WRITE:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Em Donate
already-buffered FREE pages after decode knows the iov layout
.Pq claim style .
.It
.Em Allocate-own
pages from the upper layer, optionally staging only a header window on the
socket and
.Fn readv Ns -ing
the body straight into those pages.
.El
.Pp
These APIs are independent of TCP transmit
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
.Pq see Xr rpc_svc_zerocopy 3 ;
both layers can be used together for end-to-end savings.
.Ss Path 1: donate recv buffers (claim_iov)
Use when the WRITE opaque already sits in ntirpc FREE UVs and the backend can
take ownership of those pages.
.Bl -enum
.It
Build an
.Vt iovec
describing the payload slices (as
.Dv XDR_FILLBUFS
/
.Dv XDR_IOVCOUNT
would expose).
.It
Call
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_iovs
with that iov.
On success, matching UVs are marked
.Dv UIO_FLAG_DONATED
(FREE cleared) so
.Dv XDR_DESTROY
will not
.Fn free_buffer
them.
.It
Pass the returned cookie as
.Fa priv
to the claim deleter
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_deleter
when the FSAL / Ceph path releases each slice.
The deleter frees each underlying UV base when its last donated slice is
released, then frees the cookie.
.It
If claim / submit never takes the buffers, call
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_cancel
before destroy to restore FREE and free the cookie.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_iovs
returns
.Dv NULL
(and changes nothing) if
.Fa xdrs
is not an ioq stream or any iov cannot be mapped to a FREE UV
.Pq all-or-nothing .
.Ss Path 2: upper-layer alloc + fill_payload
Use when the export wants the FSAL to own pages from the first payload byte
.Pq for example Ceph allocate_own_write_buffer .
.Pp
Register an allocator on the decode stream before filling the opaque:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef bool (*xdr_payload_alloc_fn)(void *ctx, u_int data_len,
    struct iovec **iov_out, int *iovcnt_out,
    void **cookie_out,
    void (**release_out)(void *cookie));
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa data_len
is the exact payload byte count (no XDR pad).
On success the callback returns an iovec array owned with
.Fa cookie_out ;
.Fa release_out
is invoked as
.Fn release cookie
if fill fails after alloc, or by the FSAL if it declines ownership.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xdr_ioq_set_payload_alloc(xdrs, my_alloc, my_ctx);
if (!xdr_ioq_fill_payload(xdrs, start, data_len,
			  &iov, &iovcnt, &cookie, &release)) {
	/* Do NOT fall back to XDR_FILLBUFS when a callback is set:
	 * deferred socket body is invisible to IOVCOUNT. */
	return NFS4ERR_INVAL;
}
/* Hand iov/cookie to the FSAL write path, or release(cookie). */
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xdr_ioq_fill_payload
then:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Calls the registered
.Fn payload_alloc
for
.Fa data_len
bytes.
.It
Copies any body already buffered at
.Fa start
into those pages.
.It
.Fn readv Ns s
any VC-deferred remainder from the socket into the same pages.
.It
Appends
.Dv UIO_FLAG_UPPER
UVs for deferred bytes so
.Dv XDR_SETPOS
past
.Fn RNDUP data_len
succeeds.
.It
Pulls any trailing deferred bytes (compound ops after WRITE) into a FREE UV
for continued decode.
.El
.Pp
On success,
.Fa iov_out ,
.Fa cookie_out ,
and
.Fa release_out
are owned by the caller; do not
.Fn free_buffer
the iov bases.
.Fn xdr_ioq_has_payload_alloc
reports whether a callback is installed.
.Ss Optional VC header staging (payload defer)
Without defer, recv still pulls the entire LAST_FRAG into FREE UVs before
decode.
For large WRITEs, enable staging at TCP accept
.Em before
the first recv:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/* e.g. in the accept / new-xprt hook */
svc_vc_set_payload_defer(xprt, true, 0);  /* 0 => default 256 KiB */
.Ed
.Pp
When enabled, a large LAST_FRAG stages only
.Fa hdr_stage_max
bytes into a FREE UV; the remainder stays on the socket as
.Va sx_body_remain
until
.Fn xdr_ioq_fill_payload
(or a drain) consumes it.
EPOLLIN is not rearmed until after
.Fn svc_request
returns so a concurrent recv cannot steal the body.
.Pp
.Sy Important :
enable defer at accept time.
Turning it on mid-request after EPOLLIN was rearmed races a concurrent recv
into
.Va body_remain
and can hang a worker in drain.
.Pp
Helpers:
.Bl -tag -width svc_vc_drain_body_remainXX
.It Fn svc_vc_body_remain
Bytes still deferred on the socket for the in-flight call (0 if none).
.It Fn svc_vc_readv_body
Read up to
.Fa nbytes
of deferred body into
.Fa iov
starting at byte
.Fa iov_off ;
decrements
.Va sx_body_remain .
Returns bytes read, or \-1 on error.
.It Fn svc_vc_drain_body_remain
Discard leftover deferred body into a throwaway buffer (stream sync).
Returns
.Dv true
if fully discarded (or none remained);
.Dv false
on read failure — leave the xprt for destroy rather than desync the stream.
.El
.Pp
After a failed decode with defer enabled, ntirpc drains leftovers; a failed
drain destroys the transport (fail-closed).
.Ss Typical Ganesha / FSAL flow
.Bl -enum
.It
At TCP accept, optionally
.Fn svc_vc_set_payload_defer
when the server will use allocate-own WRITE.
.It
On COMPOUND / WRITE decode, if the export wants own buffers, call
.Fn xdr_ioq_set_payload_alloc
then
.Fn xdr_ioq_fill_payload
instead of
.Dv XDR_FILLBUFS .
.It
Otherwise, after classic fillbufs, optionally
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_iovs
and pass
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_deleter
as the claim release.
.It
Never mix fill_payload fallback to FILLBUFS when a payload_alloc callback is
registered.
.El
.Ss UV ownership flags
.Bl -tag -width UIO_FLAG_DONATED
.It Dv UIO_FLAG_FREE
ntirpc-owned; destroyed with the ioq.
.It Dv UIO_FLAG_DONATED
Ownership transferred via donate; skip
.Fn free_buffer
on destroy; release via
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_deleter .
.It Dv UIO_FLAG_UPPER
Upper-layer pages from
.Fn fill_payload ;
skip
.Fn free_buffer ;
caller releases via the fill cookie.
.It Dv UIO_FLAG_REFER
Gifted reply pages (TX path); unrelated to WRITE donate, but often paired
with socket
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
on the response.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xdr_ioq_donate_iovs
returns a cookie on success, or
.Dv NULL
on failure.
.Fn xdr_ioq_fill_payload
and
.Fn xdr_ioq_has_payload_alloc
return
.Dv true
on success / when set,
.Dv false
otherwise.
.Fn svc_vc_body_remain
returns the deferred byte count.
.Fn svc_vc_readv_body
returns a byte count or \-1.
.Fn svc_vc_drain_body_remain
returns
.Dv true
on full discard,
.Dv false
on I/O failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rpc 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_create 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_zerocopy 3 ,
.Xr rpc_xdr 3 ,
.Xr readv 2
.Sh HISTORY
Upper-layer WRITE donate and deferred fill_payload support were added so
FSALs can own recv pages without an extra copy out of ntirpc FREE buffers.
